+ *
+ * ## One name, not two (AE11)
+ *
+ * `who.name` used to be fetched and then never read again — the `Who` object
+ * carried it, nothing downstream looked at it, and `<RoomChrome>` was never
+ * given a `name` prop. Watchparty's chrome fills that gap on its own, from a
+ * stored custom name, then an auto-assigned character name — never from the
+ * registry — so a viewer who had once typed a name into the chrome's own
+ * "Change character" dialog (or was auto-assigned one) saw THAT name in chat
+ * forever after, while the roster, the join line and Forge's own seat name
+ * (all fed from `member.name` — `userNameOf(req)` off `x-user-name`, server
+ * side, no client push needed) agreed with each other and not with the
+ * chrome. That is the operator's *"in the chat it says 'Eisen' but in the
+ * game it correctly sees the name as 'Bella'"*.
+ *
+ * `name={who.name ?? undefined}` below closes that for every viewer who has
+ * never customised a name in the chrome: `RoomChromeProps.name`'s own
+ * documented precedence is "the viewer's own custom name > this > an
+ * auto-assigned character name", so the registry name is now the fallback
+ * instead of a fourth, uncoordinated source.
+ *
+ * **Not closed**: a viewer who already has a stored custom name keeps it —
+ * the chrome's own precedence puts it ahead of this prop, and nothing in
+ * `RoomChromeProps` offers a way to override or clear it from outside.
